CC   This human miRNA was identified by two independent groups.  Lagos-Quintana
CC   et al. validated the presence of an 18 nt excised sequence by cloning [1].
CC   Lim et al. predicted the miR by computational methods using conservation
CC   with mouse and Fugu rubripes sequences.  Expression of the excised miR was
CC   validated in zebrafish, and the 5' end mapped by PCR [2].  The 3' ends of
CC   the reported sequences differ by 3 nt - this entry contains the longer
CC   sequence.  Lim et al. report three separate copies of this gene named
CC   mir-192-1, -2 and -3 based on 2001 human genome assemblies [2]. Subsequent
CC   assemblies suggest the presence of only one gene located on chromosome 11.
XX
